One of the most cited instances of a digital communication failure leading to a real-world issue is the Adidas email sent to participants of the Boston Marathon in 2013, saying, "Congrats, you survived the Boston Marathon". This email, sent as a congratulatory message, was sent following the Boston Marathon bombing, where three people were killed and many more were injured. The email, which was a digital communication failure due to a lack of context and understanding, was deemed to be highly offending. Adidas was forced to issue an apology following this digital communication failure.
What could have been done differently in this scenario is a more strict content check for emails. A brand should have reviewed the email sent to the participants of the marathon for possible interpretation. In addition to this, a brand team member should have understood the lack of tone in digital communications. A more personalized approach to digital communication or a more thoughtful approach, or simply avoiding digital communications, would have helped the brand avoid this digital communication failure.
